# pastebin (rung 1) — what "private" does and does not mean on this rung.
#
# Two clients, two sockets, two registered model instances. That is what makes
# this worth a scenario rather than a unit test: the question is whether one
# caller's private paste reaches another caller's listing, and an in-process
# rig with a single model instance assumes the answer.
#
# The honest answer, which this file pins rather than flatters: on rung 1
# privacy is *listing* privacy and nothing more. There is no authentication
# here at all -- no AuthModel, no session, no owner column -- so a private
# paste is unlisted, not unreadable. Anyone holding the id can read it. That
# is MicroBin's own model, and the rung README's "What to implement" says so
# by placing accounts and ownership at rung 2 (bookmarks), not here.
#
# A scenario that asserted a second client is *refused* would be asserting a
# behaviour this rung does not have and does not claim to have.
#
# Re-runnable: the listing assertions are exclusions, and no page of any size
# ever contains a private row -- the filter is in the query, not in the page.

model PasteModel

client alice

do CreatePaste content="alice's notebook" syntax=plaintext visibility=Private
expect ok capture secret=$.id

do CreatePaste content="alice's announcement" syntax=plaintext
expect ok capture public=$.id

# The owner reads her own private paste.
do GetPaste id=$secret
expect ok field content == "alice's notebook"
expect ok field visibility == Private

# ── A second connection, with its own model instance ────────────────────────
client bob

# Bob's listing carries the public paste and not the private one. The cursor
# is derived from the private id, so this is the page that would contain it.
do ListPastes cursor="$secret~"
expect ok field pastes !~ "\"id\":\"$secret\""

do ListPastes cursor="$public~"
expect ok field pastes ~ "\"id\":\"$public\""

# No page of the listing ever carries a private row, whatever its size.
do ListPastes cursor="zzzz"
expect ok field pastes !~ "\"visibility\":\"Private\""

# But the id is the only capability there is on this rung: Bob reads it.
do GetPaste id=$secret
expect ok field content == "alice's notebook"
expect ok field visibility == Private

# ...and so is deleting it. Recorded as the fact it is, not as an aspiration.
do DeletePaste id=$secret
expect ok

use alice
do GetPaste id=$secret
expect err message == "GetPaste: no such paste"
